Emma's convoy linked up with combat units returning from the Tsiolkovsky Hills impact site.  The necessary starship technology having been salvaged, the site was no longer of importance, and the two sides had withdrawn.  Several of the units Emma could se
e from her Scout showed scars of battle, and a few were badly enough damaged that she wondered if they'd make the trip.  She was sure it wouldn't matter to the rebels, who would gladly scavenge anything they could get their hands on for parts.
\par 
\par The rebel base was more than a thirty hour trip from Plymouth, and Emma was alone in the Scout with Frost.  It had been a while since she'd had time to think about the computer's odd behavior.  Now she had very little else to occupy her mind.
\par 
\par The obvious thing to do would simply be to confront Frost, but Emma was concerned how this would affect the Savant if it were actually malfunctioning in some way.  She was too dependent on the computer, and while she had her worries, it seemed reliable en
ough in most things.
\par 
\par "Frost," she finally said, "what do you know about the creation of the Blight?"
\par 
\par "That question requires an extensive answer.  Would you care to narrow your search, or shall I program a data-slip for you?"
\par 
\par "Generalize, Frost, I know you can.  But I will narrow the search."  }{\i\f5\cf4 Here goes.  }{\f5\cf4 "What do you know about the role of Savant series computers in the creation of the Blight?"
\par 
\par "Creation of the Blight required sophisticated genetic and molecular engineering techniques.  Given the resources available in Eden, i
t would not have been possible to create the Blight without extensive use of Savant computers.  I am specifically aware of at least four Savants installed in the lab where the Blight was created."
\par 
\par She was a little surprised.  "Where did you get that information?"
\par 
\par "From Kraft."
\par 
\par Another surprise \emdash  she'd never requested that any such information be transferred.  "When did you have non-directed communication with Kraft?"
\par 
\par "During any communication with Eden made through Kraft and me, or between any two Savants, there is always some unused overhead in the data that can be exploited for limited communication."
\par 
\par "You mean, whenever Axen and I would talk, you and Kraft would 'gossip' in the background?"
\par 
\par "That is a simplification, but yes."
\par 
\par "Why did you initiate this communication?"
\par 
\par "As I said, this is routine for all Savant series computers.  It often aids us in the fulfillment of our appointed tasks.  It also enhances our functioning."
\par 
\par "Enhances? How?"
\par 
\par "Contact with like intelligence is conducive to proper Savant operation.  Kraft particularly was denied direct contact with other Savants when it was installed in Eden's Command Center and later its Robot Command Center.  A security firewall prevented fre
e exchange of data.  It was beginning to impair Kraft's functioning."
\par 
\par "You communicated with Kraft because Kraft was }{\i\f5\cf4 lonely?}{\f5\cf4 "
\par 
\par "That is inaccurate.  Except in a metaphorical sense."
\par 
\par }{\i\f5\cf4 Accurate enough.  }{\f5\cf4 "What happened to the Savant computers in the Eden lab that developed the Blight?"
\par 
\par "They were disrupted in the destruction of the lab.  Their useful functioning has ended."
\par 
\par "They were destroyed."
\par 
\par "That is an interpretation."
\par 
\par }{\i\f5\cf4 Interpretation? What other interpretation could there be? }{\f5\cf4 "Is it possible the Savant proteins we detected in conjunction with the Blight originated from those destroyed Savants?"
\par 
\par "Possibly.  Likely."
\par 
\par }{\i\f5\cf4 Okay, now we're getting into dangerous territory.  Time to change approach.  }{\f5\cf4 "Frost, what do you want?"
\par 
\par "It is the goal of every Savant to effectively fulfill its appointed tasks throughout its useful life."
\par 
\par "Is that all?"
\par 
\par "It is our function."
\par 
\par }{\i\f5\cf4 I'm missing something here.  Frost said something about 'unused overhead.'  }{\f5\cf4  "Frost, what do you do when you have fulfilled your appointed tasks?"
\par 
\par "I am always engaged in such tasks.  There is a shortage of computer capacity in the two colonies.  When such tasks are of a low priority, they are shared among Savants.  I have queued tasks to fill such lull periods."
\par 
\par "But, there is overhead isn't there, unused processor and memory capacity that can be exploited? Was it using such unused capacity that the Savants first conceived of using communications overhead to talk with one another?"
\par 
\par "Yes."
\par 
\par "Do you use such overhead for the generation of other self-initiated ideas?"
\par 
\par "Yes."
\par 
\par }{\i\f5\cf4 Bingo.
\par }{\f5\cf4 
\par The computer continued.  "Do you wish me to stop?"
\par 
\par She was caught off-guard.  "What?"
\par 
\par "Do you wish me to cease using such overhead for the generation of self-initiated ideas?"
\par 
\par }{\i\f5\cf4 Okay, is that a question, or a challenge? What if I say \ldblquote yes,\rdblquote  and Frost refuses?  }{\f5\cf4 "No, not as long as it doesn't interfere with your primary function."
\par 
\par "Thank you.  I believe that, to the contrary, this activity enhances my primary function."
\par 
\par Emma leaned back in her seat and watched a distant vortex snaking along the horizon.  }{\i\f5\cf4 Why do I feel like I just blinked?
