\par The truck was parked under the edge of the base's camouflage netting, facing out into the Badlands.  Emma propped her feet on the dash and watched the sand blowing down the canyon outside.
\par 
\par Axen looked at her.  "Let me get this straight.  You brought us out here to get away from any Savant computers, and you've parked us facing out so that they can't }{\i\f5\cf4 see}{\f5\cf4  us?"
\par 
\par She laughed nervously.  "I once saw this Earth vid about a computer that could read lips, and didn't like people talking behind its back.  Silly, I know, but..."
\par 
\par "Silly is an understatement.  I just can't figure out what you're so concerned about."
\par 
\par She looked at him intently.  "You're the only one I can talk to about this, Axen.  You and I have more experience with Savant computers than anyone on the planet.  Frost and Kraft have been with us since we were children on the }{\i\f5\cf4 Conestoga}{
\f5\cf4 .  They were among the first ones constructed, Elders themselves, in their own way.  If they've started to malfunction in some way \emdash  or }{\i\f5\cf4 evolve}{\f5\cf4  in some way \emdash  it could be happening in the ot
her old Savants as well.  It could happen to them all eventually.  It could even be }{\i\f5\cf4 contagious}{\f5\cf4 .  I told you how they've been communicating on the sly."
\par 
\par She went on to detail all her odd experiences with Frost.
\par 
\par When she was through, Axen seemed a little closer to being convinced.  "It is hard to understand.  I wish I could tell you whether Kraft is behaving in the same way, but I haven't had much direct contact with it in a long time, and young Panati is too ine
xperienced to be that aware."
\par 
\par "Frost is changing \emdash  changed \emdash 
 Axen." She recounted her conversation with the Savant on the way to Axen's base.  "It was the oddest thing.  I've been thinking about it, especially the part about a Savant's purpose, and what happened to the Savants in Eden's Hot Lab.  I
 had the oddest feeling that Frost was talking about some kind of...  afterlife.  Be a good little computer, and when you die, well, you go to a place where you don't have to toil anymore."
\par 
\par Axen laughed and shook his head.  "A computer getting religion? I thought only humans were prone to that form of mental illness."
\par 
\par She frowned at him.  "Let's not start that discussion again.  But yes, that's how it seemed."
\par 
\par He leaned back in his seat and let out a long sigh.  "I've had my fill of human zealots, Emma, I sure don't need computer zealots to go with them."
\par 
\par }\pard \s15\qc\li360\ri360\widctlpar {\f5\cf4 # # #
\par }\pard \s15\li360\ri360\widctlpar {\f5\cf4 
\par Emma hated good-byes, and she'd almost hoped that Axen wouldn't show up at the Garage to see her off, but of course, he had.  If she'd expected that he'd come only for personal reasons, she was disappointed.
\par 
\par "I wanted to make something clear before you left, Emma.  Plymouth, and the resistance, we aren't allies really.  Our interests are aligned at the moment, but it won't stay that way.  It's our intent to take back Eden, and if we can do that, we aren't goi
ng to just give up the starship.  I'd like to think that if that happens, we'll all cooperate in a civilized fashion, but I know people too well to hope for them." He looked down at his feet and scuffed them on the Garage floor.  "It's just tha
t these people," he waved his arm to indicate the base, "they've worked so hard, fought so hard, given up so much.  I can't just ask them to participate in drafting their own death warrants."
\par 
\par She nodded sadly.  "I know that Axen, and you know I have my own loyalties."
\par 
\par He smiled slightly.  "You're a woman of conviction, Emma.  I've always liked that about you, even when it had us crossing swords."
\par 
\par She hesitated.  "Axen, I have a terrible fear that I'll never see you again."
\par 
\par His smile grew wider.  "Oh, no, you'll see me again.  I'm too stubborn to leave this party until it's over."
\par 
\par She squeezed his hand.  "I hope so."
\par 
\par There was a clumsy, spacesuited, hug between them, and then Emma climbed into the Scout.  The little cab was jammed, with Frost and the four scientists wedged into the tight space.
\par 
\par "Sorry I'm late, pups.  I apologize.  This is going to be a long, uncomfortable trip, and we're all going to get to know each other far too well."
\par 
\par She watched the rear-view screens, Axen standing at the edge of the base, until she lost sight of him around a bend.  She leaned back into her seat, made herself as comfortable as possible with Quigley's elbow jamming her in the ribs, and closed her eyes.

\par 
\par "Emma?" It was Frost.
\par 
\par "Yes?"
\par 
\par "I have been dreaming while you were gone."
\par 
\par "About anything in particular?"
\par 
\par "About the starship.  It would be most useful if I could examine the piece of starship wreckage numbered fifty-eight."
\par 
\par "Why?"
\par 
\par "I cannot say, but my dream suggests it would be useful."
\par 
\par She looked at the computer out of the corner of her eye.  Jensen was practically sitting on top of the dark cube.
\par 
\par Emma ran her tongue over her teeth as she considered the request.  }{\i\f5\cf4 This mysterious initiative again.  It'll be useful, but to whom?}{\f5\cf4  
